.TH TARPM "1" "September 2026" "tarpm"
.SH NAME
tarpm \- list, create, modify, and extract RPM packages without using
rpmbuild(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] [\f[B]\-?\f[R]] [\f[B]\-V\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] \f[B]\-t\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]] \f[B]\-f\f[R]
\f[I]RPMFILENAME\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] \f[B]\-x\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]] \f[B]\-f\f[R]
\f[I]RPMFILENAME\f[R] [\f[B]\-O\f[R] \f[I]DIRNAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-L\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]] [\f[B]\-S\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-H\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]] [\f[B]\-P\f[R] \f[I]DIRNAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-n\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] \f[B]\-c\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]] \f[B]\-f\f[R]
\f[I]RPMFILENAME\f[R]
[\f[B]\-H\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]] [\f[B]\-P\f[R] \f[I]DIRNAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-F\f[R] \f[I]NUMBER\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-m\f[R]] [\f[B]\-u\f[R]] [\f[B]\-g\f[R]] [\f[B]\-l\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-a\f[R]] [\f[B]\-D\f[R]] [\f[B]\-k\f[R]] \f[I]DIRECTORY\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] is a command line tool for working with RPM package
files. The interface is intended to mimic the command line program
tar(1), but with necessary options specific to RPM files. When you
extract an RPM file, a subdirectory is created matching the NEVRA of
the package, unless you name a directory with \f[B]\-O\f[R]. Inside
this directory, you will find a subdirectory called
\f[B]payload\f[R] which contains all of the files and directories in
the package.  At the same level of the \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory
you will find JSON files containing the RPM metadata.  These files may
be edited and the contents of the \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory may
be altered before creating a new RPM package.
.PP
Keep in mind the source RPM and corresponding spec file are not used
by \f[B]tarpm\f[R].  You are working with individual packages
only. Care must be taken when modifying the metadata or the payload
contents as rpm(8) may refuse to work with your newly created package.
.PP
Some of the JSON metadata is for informational purposes only and may
not be modified. See below for more details.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-?\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Display command usage information.
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Display version information.
.TP
\f[B]\-t\f[R], \f[B]\-\-list\f[R]
List the contents of the RPM package without metadata (cannot be used
with \f[B]\-c\f[R] or \f[B]\-x\f[R]).
If the package or its payload cannot be read, \f[B]tarpm\f[R] exits
non\-zero.
Control characters in a path are printed as \f[C]\\ooo\f[R] octal
escapes and a backslash is printed as \f[C]\\\\\f[R], the same as
\f[B]ls \-b\f[R].
The same goes for the paths that \f[B]\-v\f[R] prints and the paths
in warnings.
.TP
\f[B]\-x\f[R], \f[B]\-\-extract\f[R]
Extract the named RPM package on the command line (cannot be used with
\f[B]\-t\f[R] or \f[B]\-c\f[R]).
If the file list or changelog tags do not agree with each other,
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] keeps them as raw tags in \f[B]header.json\f[R] and
exits non\-zero.
.TP
\f[B]\-c\f[R], \f[B]\-\-create\f[R]
Create the named RPM package on the command line from the named
DIRECTORY contents (cannot be used with \f[B]\-t\f[R] or
\f[B]\-x\f[R]).  The DIRECTORY must contain RPM package metadata in
JSON format, like what you see when you extract an RPM.
A DIRECTORY that is missing or cannot be read is an error.
So is a \f[B]header.json\f[R] with file list tags in its tags array,
or changelog tags there that hold different numbers of values.
If writing the package fails, \f[B]tarpm\f[R] exits non\-zero and
removes the partly written file.
A package that was already there stays as it was.
A payload file that cannot be read, or that changes size during the
build, is also an error.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Print each stage of the work on standard output.
Each stage line starts with \f[B]>>>\f[R] and the details of each stage
are indented two spaces under it.
With \f[B]\-x\f[R] the stages are the package, the lead, the signature
header, the main header, the JSON files written, and the payload.
Each payload entry gets a line as it is unpacked.
When \f[B]\-L\f[R], \f[B]\-S\f[R], or \f[B]\-H\f[R] sends JSON to
standard output, the status lines go to standard error instead.
With \f[B]\-c\f[R] the details name the tags filled in, the files new
in the payload tree, the files gone from it, the data used as\-is, the
tags the format adds or drops, the files whose size or contents
changed, the payload digests, and the size of each header.
Each payload entry gets a line as it is added.
The lines for new and gone files take the place of the warnings
\f[B]\-c\f[R] prints for them without \f[B]\-v\f[R].
\f[B]\-t\f[R] always prints the long listing, so \f[B]\-v\f[R]
changes nothing there.
Other warnings go to standard error with or without \f[B]\-v\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R] FILENAME, \f[B]\-\-filename\f[R]=FILENAME
The name of the input or output RPM file.
.TP
\f[B]\-O\f[R] DIRNAME, \f[B]\-\-output\f[R]=DIRNAME
The name of the output directory when extracting an RPM.  Without this
option the extracted package goes in to a subdirectory of the
directory you are in named after the NEVRA of the package.
A NEVRA that holds a slash or a control character, or is \f[C].\f[R]
or \f[C]..\f[R], is refused and this option is required.
.RS
.PP
The directory is only made when a file actually goes in it.  Send
every piece somewhere else with \f[B]\-L\f[R], \f[B]\-S\f[R],
\f[B]\-H\f[R], and \f[B]\-P\f[R] and no output directory is made at
all.  The same goes for every directory \f[B]tarpm\f[R] writes, so you
never get an empty one left behind.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-L\f[R] PATH, \f[B]\-\-lead\f[R]=PATH
Where \f[B]lead.json\f[R] is written when extracting an RPM.  A
relative PATH is taken from the directory you are in.  A PATH ending
in a slash or naming a directory gets \f[B]lead.json\f[R] added to
it.  Any directory in PATH that is not there yet is made when the file
is written.  A PATH of a single hyphen writes the JSON to standard
output instead of to a file, and nothing is made on disk.  The RPM
lead is rebuilt from the header, so this is not read when creating an
RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-S\f[R] PATH, \f[B]\-\-signature\f[R]=PATH
Where \f[B]signature.json\f[R] is written when extracting an RPM.
PATH works the same way it does for \f[B]\-\-lead\f[R].  A PATH of a
single hyphen may only be used when extracting an RPM.  The signature
header is generated, so this is not read when creating an RPM and
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] says so on standard error if you give it anyway.
With \f[B]\-D\f[R] or \f[B]\-k\f[R] this names the
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] to take the signature digests or package
signatures from.
.TP
\f[B]\-H\f[R] PATH, \f[B]\-\-header\f[R]=PATH
Where \f[B]header.json\f[R] is written when extracting an RPM and
where it is read from when creating one.  PATH works the same way it
does for \f[B]\-\-lead\f[R].  A PATH of a single hyphen may only be
used when extracting an RPM.  Tag values that go to their own file,
such as \f[B]description.txt\f[R], sit in the same directory as
\f[B]header.json\f[R], or in the output directory when the header goes
to standard output.
.TP
\f[B]\-P\f[R] DIRNAME, \f[B]\-\-payload\f[R]=DIRNAME
Where the payload tree is written when extracting an RPM and where it
is read from when creating one.  A relative DIRNAME is taken from the
directory you are in.  When extracting, DIRNAME is created if it is
not there yet and no \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory is made in the
output directory.  When creating, DIRNAME has to be a directory that
is already there.
.TP
\f[B]\-n\f[R], \f[B]\-\-no\-payload\f[R]
Write the JSON metadata and skip the payload.  No \f[B]payload\f[R]
subdirectory is made and the cpio archive in the package is never
read.  May only be used when extracting an RPM.
.RS
.PP
This is for reading a package rather than taking it apart.  The
metadata is the same metadata a full extraction writes, so the
\f[B]files\f[R] array still describes every file the package owns.
That comes out of the header, not off the payload tree.  Tag values
that go to their own file, such as \f[B]description.txt\f[R] and the
scriptlets, are still written, since those live in the header too.
.PP
Pair it with \f[B]\-H \-\f[R] to send \f[B]header.json\f[R] to standard
output for a script to read.  Note this still makes the output
directory, because \f[B]lead.json\f[R], \f[B]signature.json\f[R], and
the tag value files have nowhere else to go.  Add \f[B]\-L \-\f[R] and
\f[B]\-S \-\f[R] and the only thing left in there is the tag value
files.
.PP
A package carrying device nodes cannot be extracted by an ordinary
user, because writing the nodes means calling mknod(2).  Reading the
metadata out of one with \f[B]\-n\f[R] works fine.
.PP
The setuid and setgid bits are not restored in the payload tree, and
device nodes get mode 0600.  The full modes stay in
\f[B]header.json\f[R].
.PP
A JSON or tag value file that is already a symlink in the output
directory is not written through, and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] exits non\-zero.
.PP
\f[B]\-P\f[R] has nothing to say when there is no payload to write, so
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] says so on standard error and carries on.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-F\f[R] NUMBER, \f[B]\-\-format\f[R]=NUMBER
The RPM format to write the package as, which is either 4 or 6.  The
default is 4.  May only be used when creating an RPM.  This is the
same thing rpm(8) does with its %_rpmformat macro.  The default does
not follow the format of the package a tree came out of, so pass
\f[B]\-F 6\f[R] to write a tree from a v6 format package back out in
the v6 format.
.RS
.PP
A v4 format signature header carries the Sha1, Sha256, Size, Md5,
and Payloadsize tags along with the reserved space rpmsign(1) writes
in to.  A v6 format one carries the Sha256 and Sha3_256 digests of
the main header and the reserved space, and nothing else.
.PP
The main header carries the payload digests the format calls for.  A
v4 format package gets Payloadsha256, Payloadsha256alt, and the
Payloaddigestalgo tag naming the algorithm.  A v6 format package gets
the Rpmformat tag, the Payloadsize and Payloadsizealt tags, and the
Payloadsha256, Payloadsha512, and Payloadsha3_256 digests along with
the alt form of each.  The alt digests cover the payload with the
compression taken back off, the others cover the payload as it lands
in the package.  The tags the other format owns are taken out, so
writing a package you extracted from one format as the other leaves
nothing behind.
.PP
Each format has main header tags the other one never uses.  tarpm
takes out the ones that do not belong, so you do not have to edit
\f[B]header.json\f[R] yourself before switching formats.  The v4
format tags are Payloaddigestalgo, Fileclass, Classdict, Size, Filesizes,
Archivesize, Filesignatures, and Filesignaturelength.  The v6 format
tags are Rpmformat, Payloadsize, Payloadsizealt, Filemimeindex, Mimedict,
Sourcenevr, Payloadsha512, Payloadsha512alt, Payloadsha3_256, and
Payloadsha3_256alt.
.PP
File sizes survive the switch.  A v4 format package counts them in
Size and Filesizes and a v6 format package counts them in Longsize
and Longfilesizes.  tarpm writes whichever pair the format wants and the
numbers stay the same.  A v4 format package uses the 64 bit tags when
a size is too large for the 32 bit ones, the same as rpm(8).  rpm(8)
counts a size of 4294967295 or more as too large.  Longsize depends on
the total size and Longfilesizes on the largest file.  A header with a
\f[B]files\f[R] array and neither Size nor Longsize gets the one the
total fits in.  A v4 format
package with Longfilesizes gets a stripped payload.  A v4 format
package too large for the 32 bit signature size tags gets Longsize and
Longarchivesize there, and the reserved space shrinks so the signature
header keeps the same size.
.PP
A v4 format package describes each file with a class and a v6 format
package describes it with a MIME type.  \f[B]tarpm\f[R] works out the
one the new format needs from the file in the payload tree.  See FILE CLASSES
and MIME TYPES below.
.PP
One thing does not survive.  The rpmlib() entries in
\f[B]dependencies\f[R] are left alone, so a package you convert to
v6 format keeps the ones rpm(8) only adds for v4 format.  Edit
\f[B]dependencies\f[R] yourself if you do not want them.
.PP
Sourcenevr does survive, even though it is a v6 format tag a v4
format package cannot carry.  It is the Sourcerpm value with the \(dq.src.rpm\(dq
ending taken off, so \f[B]tarpm\f[R] works it out again on the way
back to the v6 format.
.PP
The lead records the format in its major version, which is 3 for the
v4 format and 4 for the v6 format.  Switching formats does not change a payload compressor
the metadata names.  A package whose metadata names none gets the
rpm(8) default for the format.  The payload of a v6 format package is
a stripped cpio archive.  See PAYLOAD below.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-mtime\f[R]
Take the timestamp of each file from the file in the payload tree
rather than from the \f[B]mtime\f[R] key in \f[B]header.json\f[R].
May only be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-u\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-user\f[R]
Take the owner of each file from the file in the payload tree rather
than from the \f[B]user\f[R] key in \f[B]header.json\f[R].  May only
be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-g\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-group\f[R]
Take the group of each file from the file in the payload tree rather
than from the \f[B]group\f[R] key in \f[B]header.json\f[R].  May only
be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-linkto\f[R]
Take the target of each symlink from the symlink in the payload tree
rather than from the \f[B]linkto\f[R] key in \f[B]header.json\f[R].
The \f[B]size\f[R] of a symlink follows its target, so that is taken
from the payload tree too.  May only be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-a\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-all\f[R]
The same as giving \f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-u\f[R], \f[B]\-g\f[R], and
\f[B]\-l\f[R] together.  May only be used when creating an RPM.
.PP
The \f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-u\f[R], \f[B]\-g\f[R], \f[B]\-l\f[R], and
\f[B]\-a\f[R] options may be given in any combination, so you can take
the owner off the payload tree without taking the group off it.  Each
one only speaks for the value it names.  An entry with no file in the
payload tree, such as a %ghost, is left as \f[B]header.json\f[R] has
it.  \f[B]tarpm\f[R] says so on standard error each time it takes a
value off a file, but only when the file and \f[B]header.json\f[R] do
not already agree.
.TP
\f[B]\-D\f[R], \f[B]\-\-keep\-digests\f[R]
Use the digests in \f[B]header.json\f[R] and
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] as they are instead of recomputing them.
This covers the file digests in the file list, the Payloadsha256 family
of tags in \f[B]header.json\f[R], and the Md5, Sha1, Sha256, and
Sha3_256 tags in \f[B]signature.json\f[R].  The values are not
checked, so whatever digest the JSON carries goes straight in to the
package.  A digest missing from the JSON still gets one computed.  A
DIRECTORY with no \f[B]signature.json\f[R] gets every signature
digest computed.  A signature digest is only kept when the format puts
that tag in the signature header.  \f[B]\-S\f[R] names a
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] outside the DIRECTORY.  This is for producing
packages with known bad digests to test tools that verify them.  May
only be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-k\f[R], \f[B]\-\-keep\-signatures\f[R]
Copy the package signatures in \f[B]signature.json\f[R] in to the
package.  These are the Dsa, Rsa, Openpgp, Pgp, Gpg, and Pgp5 tags.
A tag with no value is left out.  If a tag appears more than once,
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] warns and keeps the first.  Pgp, Gpg, and Pgp5 are
left out of a v6 format package with a warning.  The values are
copied as they are and not checked.  The room they take comes out of
the reserved space in the signature header, the same as rpmsign(1).  A
DIRECTORY with no
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] gets no package signatures.
\f[B]\-S\f[R] names a \f[B]signature.json\f[R] outside the
DIRECTORY.  A signature only verifies if the header and payload it
covers come out byte for byte the same as in the signed package.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] warns when the Sha256 of the new main header differs
from the one in \f[B]signature.json\f[R].
Without \f[B]\-D\f[R] the signature digests are still computed.  May
only be used when creating an RPM.
.PP
Similar to tar(1), you may run options together, such as
\f[B]\-xvf\f[R] or \f[B]\-cvf\f[R]. Likewise, the leading hyphen on
combined options like this is optional (in order to make
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] more syntax compatible with tar(1)).
Without the hyphen, the combined options must be the first argument,
such as \f[B]tarpm xvf\f[R] FILENAME.  A letter that is not an option
is an error.
.PP
An empty DIRNAME for \f[B]\-O\f[R] or \f[B]\-P\f[R] is an error.
.SH METADATA
RPM metadata is extracted by \f[B]tarpm\f[R] and written to JSON
files.  When you create a package with \f[B]tarpm\f[R], only
\f[B]header.json\f[R] must be present in the DIRECTORY serving as the
source for the package.  \f[B]lead.json\f[R] and
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] are optional.  We never read
\f[B]lead.json\f[R] and only \f[B]\-D\f[R] and \f[B]\-k\f[R] read
\f[B]signature.json\f[R].
Fields that are read\-only are marked with a read\-only string set to
true. The JSON files are:
.TP
\f[B]lead.json\f[R]
The RPM lead.
Obsolete, only shown for informational purposes. \f[B]tarpm\f[R]
recreates the lead when it creates a package.  The RPM lead is the
first 96 bytes of any RPM file.
.RS
.PP
Because we build the lead from the Name, Epoch, Version, Release, and
Sourcepackage tags in \f[B]header.json\f[R], the DIRECTORY you create
a package from does not need a \f[B]lead.json\f[R] at all.  If one is
there we leave it alone, so editing it changes nothing in the package
we write.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]signature.json\f[R]
The RPM signature.
Marked with a magic number followed by the number of records and the
size of the data area. Information is stored in key=value manner but
value may be an array. The contents of the RPM signature is read\-only
as \f[B]tarpm\f[R] will recalculate all of the values when creating an
RPM.
.RS
.PP
The tags in \f[B]signature.json\f[R] are presented for informational
purposes only.  When you create an RPM with \f[B]tarpm\f[R], we build
the signature header from scratch for the format you ask for with
\f[B]\-F\f[R], so the DIRECTORY does not need a
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] at all and we do not read one that is there,
unless \f[B]\-D\f[R] asks for the digests it carries or
\f[B]\-k\f[R] asks for the package signatures.  Otherwise it is
just like the lead.  To reiterate this point, the tags in
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] carry a read\-only marker, the same as in the
\f[B]lead.json\f[R] file.
.PP
A package built by rpmbuild(1) and then signed by rpmsign(1) carries
signature tags that only rpmsign(1) can make, which are Dsa, Rsa,
Pgp, Gpg, Pgp5, and Openpgp.  \f[B]tarpm\f[R] reads all
of them out of a package and writes them to \f[B]signature.json\f[R]
so you can see what the package carries, but it leaves every one of
them out of a package it creates because making them takes the
private signing key.  Run rpmsign(1) on the new package to sign it
again.  \f[B]\-k\f[R] copies them from \f[B]signature.json\f[R] in
to the new package as they are.
.PP
The file signatures rpmsign(1) makes are different.  Filesignatures,
Filesignaturelength, Veritysignatures, and Veritysignaturealgo sign
each file and not the package, so they still hold after a rebuild.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] does not write them to \f[B]signature.json\f[R].  The
per-file lists go to the signature and verity keys of the
\f[B]files\f[R] array in \f[B]header.json\f[R], and
Veritysignaturealgo goes to its \f[B]tags\f[R] array.  We work
Filesignaturelength out again from the signatures.  A package we
create gets them back in its signature header, in either format.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]header.json\f[R]
The RPM header.
Contains the metadata that most users are expecting to find. Things
from the spec file, changelog information, dependency information, and
so on. It is also marked with a magic number followed by the number of
records and the size of the data area. Information is stored in
key=value manner but value may be an array.
.PP
The contents of \f[B]lead.json\f[R] and \f[B]signature.json\f[R] files
cannot be modified, but might be interesting to look at or use for
verification purposes.
.PP
The contents of the \f[B]header.json\f[R] file can be modified.
.SH HEADER.JSON
The \f[B]header.json\f[R] file is the main metadata file that can be
mostly modified. Fields that must remain untouched are magic,
reserved, and the Headerimmutable tags. The JSON file is broken up in
to arrays for easier editing via programming languages or automation
tools. The objective with the JSON structure is to avoid needing to
parse information like you may have to do reading filenames or spec
files.
.PP
The arrays in \f[B]header.json\f[R] containing metadata:
.TP
\f[B]tags\f[R]
Information seen in spec files such as Name, Version, and Release. You
will also find things automatically added by rpmbuild if the package
was generated with rpmbuild.  The tags array is a series of key=value
dicts. Each array entry contains a dict and each dict contains tag,
type, and value or file. The tag corresponds to an RPMTAG_ value from
/usr/include/rpm/rpmtag.h. The value is the actual information. The
type corresponds to the types defined in /usr/include/rpm/rpmtag.h. If
the type is an array, the value will be an array of that type.
A string tag needs a JSON string for its value. tarpm will not create
a package from one holding a number, a boolean, or null. An i18n string
tag holds the same, or an object of locale to string when it has
translations, such as {\(dqC\(dq: \(dqhello\(dq, \(dqde\(dq: \(dqhallo\(dq}. Every locale
has to be in the Headeri18ntable tag. A translated Description keeps
each string in a file, so its \f[B]file\f[R] key holds the object,
with \f[B]description.txt\f[R] for the first locale and
\f[B]description.\f[R]\f[I]locale\f[R]\f[B].txt\f[R] for the others.
A char tag holds a string of one or more characters. tarpm will not
create a package from a type it does not know. That includes (null)
and (unknown), which it writes on extract for an RPM_NULL_TYPE tag or
a type rpm does not define.
.PP
If an entry in the tags array contains a file key rather than value,
then that key corresponds to a text file in the extracted RPM
directory. Some tags are written to text files for easier editing. The
Description, all scriptlets, and the Spec (for source RPMs only) are
all written to text files rather than placed directly in the JSON
file.  A header you write by hand may give any of those a value key
instead and keep the text in the JSON file.
.TP
\f[B]dependencies\f[R]
RPM dependency information is written to the \f[B]dependencies\f[R]
dict. There is one dict entry (key) for each type of dependency which
is any of provides, requires, conflicts, obsoletes, recommends,
suggests, supplements, and enhances. Any other key is left out of the
package with a warning. The value for each key is an
array of dicts where each array entry is a dict consisting of at least
name and optionally comparison, version, and sense_flags. The
structure looks like this:
 :
\(lqdependencies\(rq: {
.IP
.EX
 \(dqDEP_TYPE\(dq: [
.EE
.IP
.EX
     {
.EE
.IP
.EX
         \(dqname\(dq: \(dqVALUE\(dq,
.EE
.IP
.EX
         \(dqcomparison\(dq: \(dqVALUE\(dq,
.EE
.IP
.EX
         \(dqversion\(dq: \(dqVALUE\(dq,
.EE
.IP
.EX
         \(dqsense_flags\(dq: [
.EE
.IP
.EX
             \(dqFLAG\(dq, ...
.EE
.IP
.EX
         ]
.EE
.IP
.EX
     }, ...
.EE
} :
.PP
All dependencies must have at least a name. This can be given
explicitly in a spec file and correspond to a file path or a package
name. Or it can be autogenerated by rpmbuild. A dependency may also
have a comparison which can be =, <, >, <=, or >=. If it has a
comparison operator it also needs a version string. The sense_flags
are dependency flags found in the header that do not strictly map to a
comparison operator. \f[B]tarpm\f[R] converts these to string
representation and places them in the sense_flags array. You can find
a list of these in /usr/include/rpm/rpmds.h as the RPMSENSE_
macros. Most of these are autogenerated by rpmbuild. Bits with no
name go in the array as one decimal number so a round trip keeps
them.
.PP
You may change these dependency values in the JSON metadata, but keep
in mind that \f[B]tarpm\f[R] does no validation or checking other
than to make sure the metadata types are correct. It will put whatever
you tell it to put in the header. If you wish to manually create an
RPM, consider using the /usr/lib/rpm/find\-requires and
/usr/lib/rpm/find\-provides tools to generate this information for the
software you are packaging. That will align with what an RPM database
is expecting.
.TP
\f[B]files\f[R]
The file list of the package. Each array entry is a dict describing
one file, directory, symlink, or device node the package owns. RPM
spreads this information over a couple of dozen parallel arrays in the
header and stores most of it as numbers and bitfields.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] gathers all of that in to one dict per file and turns
the numbers in to names, so you edit one entry rather than count your
way through several arrays. A key is left off entirely when the file
has nothing to say for it, so most entries carry only some of these:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]path\f[R]
The absolute path the file has once the package is installed. This is
the only key every entry carries. It is also where the file sits
inside the \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory.
.TP
\f[B]size\f[R]
The number of bytes in a regular file, or the length of the target of
a symlink. Other kinds of file do not carry this. It comes from
Filesizes or Longfilesizes, whichever the package has. On create,
tarpm writes Longfilesizes for a v6 format package and for a v4 format
package with a file of 4294967295 bytes or more, and Filesizes
otherwise.
.TP
\f[B]mode\f[R]
The permission bits as a four digit octal string, such as
\(dq0755\(dq. The file type bits are not in here, they are in
\f[B]type\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]type\f[R]
What kind of thing the entry is. One of file, dir, symlink, chardev,
blockdev, pipe, or socket.
.TP
\f[B]mtime\f[R]
The modification time as an ISO 8601 timestamp in UTC, such as
\(dq2026\-07\-16T00:00:00Z\(dq. RPM stores this as a time_t.
.TP
\f[B]user\f[R], \f[B]group\f[R]
The owner and group names. RPM records names here rather than numbers,
so the same package works on systems that number their users
differently.
.TP
\f[B]rdev\f[R]
The device number, for character and block device nodes only.
RPM stores it in 16 bits, so a number above 65535 is an error.
.TP
\f[B]device\f[R]
The device the file came off when the package was built. This is
almost always 1.
.TP
\f[B]digest\f[R]
The digest of the contents of a regular file as a hex string. The
algorithm is the one the Filedigestalgo tag names, which rpm defaults
to md5. Entries with no contents to digest do not carry this.
.TP
\f[B]linkto\f[R]
The target of a symlink.
.TP
\f[B]inode\f[R]
A number RPM gives out itself starting at 1. Files that share a number
are hard links of one another. These are not real inode numbers.
A file with no inode key is a hard link of the files it is linked to
in the payload tree. When one of those files has an inode key, the new
file joins its group. Two files with different inode keys are never
hard links of each other, even when they are linked in the payload
tree.
.TP
\f[B]class\f[R]
What kind of thing the file holds, in the style of file(1), such as
\(dqASCII text\(dq or a long ELF description. RPM only records a class
for files it recognizes, so plenty of entries do not carry this. Only
a v4 format package records classes. See FILE CLASSES below.
.TP
\f[B]mime\f[R]
The MIME type of the file, such as \(dqtext/plain\(dq or
\(dqinode/directory\(dq. Only a v6 format package records MIME types. See
MIME TYPES below.
.TP
\f[B]langs\f[R]
An array of the languages of the file, such as the language of a
translated man page. RPM stores these as one string with bars between
the languages and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] splits it in to an array.
.TP
\f[B]colors\f[R]
An array naming the ELF class of the file, either Elf32 or Elf64. RPM
uses this to work out which of two packages wins when a multilib
install puts a 32 bit and a 64 bit copy of the same path on the
system.
.TP
\f[B]flags\f[R]
An array of the markings the file got in the spec file, such as
config, doc, ghost, license, readme, missingok, noreplace, or
artifact. RPM stores these as a bitfield.
.TP
\f[B]verifyflags\f[R]
An array naming what rpm(8) checks when verifying the file, such as
filedigest, filesize, mtime, mode, user, group, linkto, rdev, and
caps.  RPM stores these as a bitfield too.  An entry carrying no
verifyflags key at all gets every check, which is where rpmbuild
starts each file.  Write an empty array for a file that should be
checked for none of these.  tarpm writes an empty array when it
extracts a file like that.
.TP
\f[B]provides\f[R]
An array of the dependencies this one file generated. Each entry looks
like a dependency from the \f[B]dependencies\f[R] dict with a type key
added saying which kind it is. Despite the name, requires show up here
as well as provides, because rpm records both against the file the
generators read them out of. The order matters and \f[B]tarpm\f[R]
keeps it, since rpm points at a run of these per file.
.TP
\f[B]signature\f[R]
The IMA file signature rpmsign(1) made for the file, as a hex string.
RPM keeps these in the Filesignatures tag of the signature header.
.TP
\f[B]verity\f[R]
The fs\-verity signature rpmsign(1) made for the file, as a base64
string. RPM keeps these in the Veritysignatures tag of the signature
header. The hash algorithm is the Veritysignaturealgo tag in the
\f[B]tags\f[R] array, 1 for sha256 and 2 for sha512. Without it
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] writes 1, the same as rpmsign(1). A value that is
not a number gets a warning and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] writes 1.
.PP
A file with no signature has neither key. \f[B]tarpm\f[R] writes these
to the signature header in both formats. The room they take comes out
of the reserved space, so the signature header keeps its size, the
same as rpmsign(1) does. Signatures too large for the reserved space
leave it alone and the signature header grows. A file whose contents
changed loses both, because they sign the old contents. A source
package never gets them.
.PP
An older rpmsign(1) put the file signatures in the main header of a v4
format package. Extracting one of those moves them to the signature
keys as well, and drops Filesignatures and Filesignaturelength from the
\f[B]tags\f[R] array. A package \f[B]tarpm\f[R] creates from it has
them in the signature header, in either format.
.PP
rpm(8) turns away a package with file signatures in both headers. If
you put a Filesignatures tag in the \f[B]tags\f[R] array yourself, a
v4 format package keeps it and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] leaves the signature
keys out of the signature header.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]changelog\f[R]
The changelog information from the spec file is stored as an array in
the \f[B]header.json\f[R] file. It is an array of dicts and each dict
corresponds to one changelog block from the spec file. Each dict
contains a timestamp key, a name key, and a text key. The timestamp
value is the date you would see on the changelog line, such as \(lqTue
Jul 21 2026\(rq. A time other than noon UTC is written with the time
of day, such as \(lqThu Oct 06 06:48:39 UTC 2016\(rq. Internally these
are stored as time_t values in the header, but tarpm converts them to
the human readable format. The name
value is a string containing everything after the date on the
changelog line. Usually this is the packager\(cqs name, email address,
a hyphen, and the version\-release value of that update (but without
the dist tag), but this is just a common convention. The text value is
an array of strings containing everything for that changelog
entry. The actual value in the header is one long string, but tarpm
splits that on newlines and presents it as an array in the JSON file
for easier editing. A blank line is an empty string in the array. The
entire array is joined together with newlines when constructing an
RPM, so the text comes back exactly as it was.
.SH MINIMAL PACKAGES
You do not have to extract a package to create one.  A directory
holding a \f[B]header.json\f[R] file that you construct and a
\f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory is enough. \f[B]tarpm\f[R] walks the
payload tree, builds the whole file list from what it finds, and fills
in the header tags it needs for the RPM file format you are creating.
.PP
A minimum \f[B]header.json\f[R] file requires \f[B]Name\f[R],
\f[B]Version\f[R], and \f[B]Release\f[R]. If any of those are missing
from the JSON file, tarpm treats that as an error.  \f[B]License\f[R]
has no sensible default either, so \f[B]tarpm\f[R] warns when a header
is missing the License tag.  \f[B]Summary\f[R] is worth writing as
well, since queries look wrong without it.
.PP
These are the tags \f[B]tarpm\f[R] fills in and the value each one
gets.  A tag the header already carries is never touched, so a tree you
extracted goes back out unchanged:
.TP
\f[B]Os\f[R]
linux
.TP
\f[B]Arch\f[R]
The machine name from uname(2), or src for a source package
.TP
\f[B]Payloadformat\f[R]
cpio, which is the only format rpm accepts (for now)
.TP
\f[B]Payloadcompressor\f[R], \f[B]Payloadflags\f[R]
gzip at level 9 for a v4 format package or a source package, zstd at
level 19 for a v6 format binary package.
These match the rpm(8) defaults.
A compressor the metadata names is kept in either format.
A v6 format package that names a compressor other than zstd gets
level 9.
The names are gzip, bzip2, xz, lzma, zstd, and none.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] refuses any other name.
none gives an uncompressed payload.
rpm(8) has no name for this, so the package carries no
Payloadcompressor tag and Payloadflags is 0, the same as rpmbuild(8)
writes.
Extracting an uncompressed package writes none to
\f[B]header.json\f[R].
A gzip payload carries an mtime of 0 in its gzip header, the same as
rpm writes.
An xz payload carries a SHA-256 check, the same as rpm writes.
xz takes levels 0 to 9.
Payloadflags is the compression level, with an optional T and thread
count after it, such as 3T or 19T8.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] uses the level and ignores the thread count.
Any other value is an error, and so is a level the compressor does not
take.
.TP
\f[B]Sourcerpm\f[R]
(none) for a binary package, left off for a source package
.TP
\f[B]Sourcenevr\f[R]
The Sourcerpm value with the \(dq.src.rpm\(dq ending taken off.  Only
a v6 format binary package gets this, and only when there is a source
package to name
.TP
\f[B]Filedigestalgo\f[R]
sha256, unless the tree already describes its files, where a missing
tag means md5 and the digests already in it were made that way
.TP
\f[B]Buildtime\f[R]
Now
.TP
\f[B]Buildhost\f[R]
The name from gethostname(2)
.TP
\f[B]Summary\f[R], \f[B]Description\f[R]
The Name value
.TP
\f[B]Headerimmutable\f[R]
The region trailer, without which rpm reads the package as a v3
format one and says the v3 format is deprecated.
tarpm refuses a header with more than one region tag, or with a
\f[B]Headersignatures\f[R] tag, which belongs in the signature header.
.PP
The package also gets a Provide dependency for itself, which is its
name at its own EVR, unless the \f[B]dependencies\f[R] dict already
provides the name.
.PP
So the smallest tree that makes an installable package is a
\f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory holding the files and this
\f[B]header.json\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqtags\(dq: [
    { \(dqtag\(dq: \(dqName\(dq,    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,      \(dqvalue\(dq: \(dqhello\(dq },
    { \(dqtag\(dq: \(dqVersion\(dq, \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,      \(dqvalue\(dq: \(dq1.0\(dq },
    { \(dqtag\(dq: \(dqRelease\(dq, \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,      \(dqvalue\(dq: \(dq1\(dq },
    { \(dqtag\(dq: \(dqSummary\(dq, \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqi18n string\(dq, \(dqvalue\(dq: \(dqA greeting\(dq },
    { \(dqtag\(dq: \(dqLicense\(dq, \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,      \(dqvalue\(dq: \(dqMIT\(dq }
  ]
}
.EE
.PP
Running \f[B]tarpm \-cvf hello.rpm \-F 6 ./tree\f[R] over that then
gives you a package rpm(8) installs.
.PP
A package with nothing in its payload tree is fine too. rpm takes a
package with no files at all, so \f[B]tarpm\f[R] simply leaves the
file list tags out of the header.  The same goes for a tree you
extracted and then emptied, where every file list entry drops out.
.SH PAYLOAD
The \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory contains the RPM payload
contents. You may modify this subdirectory by adding files, removing
files, and changing what is in them. \f[B]tarpm\f[R] will update the
file metadata in the header based on the contents of this
subdirectory, so it is not necessary to manually update the
\f[B]files\f[R] array in \f[B]header.json\f[R].
.PP
The payload in the package itself is a cpio archive, written one of
two ways.  The usual one is newc, which every cpio tool reads.  A
package whose header carries Longfilesizes gets what rpm calls a
stripped archive instead, with a magic of 07070X and a file index in
each entry in place of the name, the mode, and the timestamps, since
the main header already holds all of that.  Every v6 format package
is written that way, and so is a v4 format package holding a file too
large to count in 32 bits.
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] reads both.  libarchive is quick but only knows
newc, and librpm knows both.  A package whose header carries
Longfilesizes goes straight to librpm.  Any other package goes to
libarchive first, and to librpm only when libarchive cannot read a
single entry.  Each entry librpm reads is written straight to the
payload tree, so nothing goes to \f[B]$TMPDIR\f[R].  An entry that fails after others went in is reported once
and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] exits non-zero without trying the second
reader.  \f[B]tarpm\f[R] writes a stripped
archive whenever the header carries Longfilesizes and newc otherwise,
the same as rpm(8).
.PP
When you create a package, \f[B]tarpm\f[R] walks the payload tree and
the file list together and reconciles them:
.TP
Files you removed
An entry naming a file that is no longer in the payload tree is left
out of the new package. A %ghost file is never in the payload to begin
with, so those are always kept.
.TP
Files you added
A file in the payload tree that the file list does not name gets an
entry of its own, filled in from the file itself. The directories
leading to a file the list already names do not get entries, since the
payload tree needs those to hold the files under them but the package
never owned them.
.TP
Files you changed
The type, the size, the symlink target, the device number, the digest,
the class, and the MIME type are taken from the file on disk rather
than from the entry. Swapping a file for a symlink, or for any other kind of thing,
rewrites the rest of the entry to suit the new type.
.TP
Keys you left out
An entry that is missing a key the header needs gets one, read off the
file in the payload tree. The owner, the group, and the device are not
things a file can answer, so those fall back to root, root, and 1.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] says so on standard error each time it fills one in.
.PP
The rest of an entry comes from the JSON and is written to the header
as you wrote it. That includes the permission bits, the mtime, the
owner, the group, the flags, the verify flags, the languages, and the
colors. Changing the mode or the timestamp of a file in the payload
tree does \f[B]not\f[R] change the package, because the entry already
says what those should be. Edit the \f[B]mode\f[R] and
\f[B]mtime\f[R] keys in \f[B]header.json\f[R] instead. A file you add
to the payload tree is the exception, since it has no entry yet, so
everything about it including its mode and its timestamp is read off
the file.
.PP
The \f[B]\-\-payload\-mtime\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-user\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-payload\-group\f[R], and \f[B]\-\-payload\-linkto\f[R]
options turn that around for the value each one names. With
\f[B]\-\-payload\-mtime\f[R], for instance, the timestamp of a file in
the payload tree is what lands in the header and the \f[B]mtime\f[R]
key is disregarded. See \f[B]OPTIONS\f[R] above.
.SH FILE CLASSES
RPM records what kind of thing each file holds, in the style of
file(1), and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] shows it as the \f[B]class\f[R] key on a
file list entry. Internally this is a table of the class strings in
the package and one index per file in to that table.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] builds both of those for you.
.PP
The class is worked out the same way rpmbuild(1) works it out. A file
that is not a regular file or a symlink gets a fixed name from its
type. A handful of path endings, such as .h and .png, get an answer
without looking at the file at all. Anything under /dev/ gets no class
because those stand in for device nodes the system makes. Everything
else goes to libmagic, the library behind file(1).
.PP
Whatever comes back is only kept if it holds one of the strings rpm
looks for, so a great many files end up with no class at all and no
\f[B]class\f[R] key. That is normal and does not mean anything is
wrong.
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] only works the class out again when the file may have
changed, which is when the file is new in the payload tree, when its
type changed, or when its contents no longer match the digest the
entry came in with. A file you did not touch keeps the class the
original package gave it.
.PP
Note that rpm(8) invents a class at query time when the one in the
package is empty, making one up from the file mode or, for a symlink,
from the link target. An empty class is therefore not always what
\(dqrpm \-qp \-\-queryformat\(dq shows you.
.PP
All of this is v4 format only.  A v6 format package records a MIME
type per file rather than a class, so a file list out of a v6 format
package carries no \f[B]class\f[R] key at all.  When you write a v4
format package from it,
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] works out the class of each entry that has a
\f[B]mime\f[R] key and no \f[B]class\f[R] key.
.SH MIME TYPES
A v6 format package records the MIME type of each file in place of a
class,
and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] shows it as the \f[B]mime\f[R] key on a file
list entry. Internally this is the Mimedict table of the MIME types in
the package and the Filemimeindex index per file in to that table.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] builds both of those for you. A source package
carries neither.
.PP
The MIME type is worked out the same way rpmbuild(1) works it out. A
regular file or a symlink with one of the path endings that gets a
class without looking at the file gets a MIME type the same way, such
as text/x-c for .h. Everything else goes to libmagic, including
directories, symlinks, and the files under /dev/. Every MIME type is
kept. A file that is not there has none and a file libmagic cannot
read is application/octet-stream.
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] works the MIME type out again under the same
conditions as the class. A file you did not touch keeps the MIME type
the original package gave it.
.PP
A file list out of a v4 format package carries no \f[B]mime\f[R]
key. When you write a v6 format package from it, \f[B]tarpm\f[R] works out the MIME
type of each entry that has no \f[B]mime\f[R] key.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[B]rpmbuild\f[R](1), \f[B]rpmsign\f[R](1), \f[B]tar\f[R](1),
\f[B]file\f[R](1), \f[B]rpm\f[R](8), \f[B]rpm2cpio\f[R](8)
